`reportData.dataSource` is treated like a query configuration object (`object/resource/filter/sort/limit`), but `ReportSchema.dataSource` in `@object-ui/types` is typed as a `DataSource` adapter interface (methods like `find`, `create`, etc.) and would not have these properties. If reports are expected to carry a serializable query config, this branch should use a dedicated config type/property (to avoid shadowing the adapter passed via props) so that reports with a real `dataSource` instance don’t get short-circuited into the “missing object/resource” warning path.
```suggestion
    // If report has a dataSource configuration, fetch data using it.
    // Prefer a dedicated `dataSourceConfig` field; fall back to a legacy
    // `dataSource` config object only when it does not look like a DataSource adapter.
    const dataSourceConfig =
      (reportData as any).dataSourceConfig ??
      (reportData.dataSource &&
        typeof reportData.dataSource === 'object' &&
        typeof (reportData.dataSource as any).find !== 'function'
          ? (reportData.dataSource as any)
          : undefined);

    if (dataSourceConfig) {
      const fetchDataFromSource = async () => {
        setDataLoading(true);
        try {
          // Use the dataSource configuration to fetch data
          const resource = dataSourceConfig.object || dataSourceConfig.resource;
          if (!resource) {
            console.warn('ReportView: dataSource config missing object/resource property');
            setReportRuntimeData([]);
            return;
          }

          const result = await dataSource.find(resource, {
            $filter: dataSourceConfig.filter,
            $orderby: dataSourceConfig.sort,
            $top: dataSourceConfig.limit,
          });

          setReportRuntimeData(result.data || []);
